      "As of 28 September the proposed state channel exists on paper, and the two governments describe it differently. Xi Jinping paid a state visit to Washington from 23 to 25 September. The White House fact sheet says the two countries established the U.S.-China Super Intelligence Dialogue to exchange views on risks and benefits, that the next exchange will occur by November 2026, and that they agreed to establish a bilateral communication channel for SI incidents; it also records the two leaders agreeing to use the term super intelligence in place of artificial intelligence. The Chinese Ministry of Foreign Affairs readout, listing eight deliverables and understandings, describes what reads as the same two mechanisms as the China-U.S. AI Dialogue and a bilateral communication channel for AI incidents, with the next exchange in November 2026. The Chinese readout does not use the words super intelligence anywhere. Neither document names an agency, a threshold or a venue, and UPI reports it remained unclear what kind of incident would trigger the dialogue.",
      "Chips stayed outside the arrangement. The US Trade Representative said export controls on advanced chips and chipmaking equipment were not on the agenda, and Trump said afterwards that the United States is not going to be putting on brakes. Roll Call reported that Senate Armed Services Chairman Roger Wicker agreed to fold Senator Tom Cotton's chip location-tracking bill into the managers amendment to the fiscal 2027 defence authorisation, alongside bills from Senators Pete Ricketts and Jim Banks, with House Foreign Affairs Committee counterparts already reported out; none has passed either chamber and Senate Democrats blocked the authorisation over unrelated objections. On the domestic-supply side Alibaba unveiled the Zhenwu V900 accelerator and set a target of more than 20GW of data-centre capacity by 2032. No named Chinese company has produced records contradicting the distillation figures, and no US agency has published its own analysis."
