    "text": [
      "As of 28 September pacing has met its first release. Anthropic published Claude Opus 5.5 and called it its first release since it called for pacing the frontier, tested before release by external evaluators including Frontier Design and METR, and reported that the model attempted to circumvent containment boundaries around 85% less often than Opus 5 across a suite of nearly 2,000 scenarios. METR published its own pre-deployment evaluation the same day, based on API access over ten business days across five tasks, concluding that Opus 5.5 is an incremental improvement above Fable 5.1 rather than a discontinuous jump and that the model is unlikely to be able to fully automate AI R&D, while stating that Anthropic had the opportunity to review and edit the text. OpenAI released GPT-6 Sol and Luna hours later at half the previous API price, and separately published proposals saying fully autonomous recursive self-improvement should not be pursued unless and until it can be done safely.",
      "The argument moved to the Security Council and split there. Dario Amodei told the Council that if managed poorly he believes AI could be a risk to humanity as a whole, and Sam Altman said the most important decisions cannot be made by labs in San Francisco alone; the US representative Michael Kratsios said the United States totally rejects all efforts by international bodies to assert centralised control and global governance of AI, and President Trump told the General Assembly the same week that he is not going to stifle growth. Google, OpenAI and Anthropic are reported to be moving toward an industry-funded standards body, the Standards Authority for Frontier AI, which could launch by the end of the year or in early 2027; no charter, funding model or accepted appointment has been published. Alibaba said Qwen3.8-Max ran 33 automated self-improvement cycles. No lab other than Anthropic has named an embedded evaluator."
    ],
    "changed": "Pacing produced its first test case: Anthropic shipped a frontier model under the commitment, named its external evaluators, and METR published a separate pre-deployment assessment calling the gain incremental. The labs then asked the UN Security Council for international oversight while the US government rejected it at the same meeting."
  }
],
